<Base title="Poems | Syneidesis" lang="en">
    <h1>Poems</h1>

    <section class="lista-textos">
        <article>
            <h2>
                <a href="/en/article.html?categoria=poems&texto=brasil-ame">
                    Brazil, Love Me, or Leave Me
                </a>
            </h2>
            <p>
                A jingoistic poem tinged with satire, serving as a critique of
                the Brazilian military dictatorship.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <h2>
                <a href="/en/article.html?categoria=poems&texto=noite">Night</a>
            </h2>
            <p>
                An experimental poem, emerging from the author's own
                subconscious.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <h2>
                <a href="/en/article.html?categoria=poems&texto=noitew">
                    Good Night, Dear Werther!
                </a>
            </h2>
            <p>
                A poem inspired by the letter of 21 August in The Sorrows of
                Young Werther, concerning the protagonist's dream of his
                beloved, Charlotte.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <h2>
                <a href="/en/article.html?categoria=poems&texto=prazeres">
                    The Pleasures of Young Werther
                </a>
            </h2>
            <p>
                A poem set in an alternate universe of The Sorrows of Young
                Werther, in which the protagonist pours out his emotions after
                kissing his beloved.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <h2>
                <a href="/en/article.html?categoria=poems&texto=ultimo-dia-werther">
                    Antemortem
                </a>
            </h2>
            <p>
                A poem inspired by the description of the night of Werther's
                death. It portrays his chaotic state of mind in his final
                moments, as he seeks solace in his final act.
            </p>
        </article>
    </section>
</Base>
